WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.241 --> 00:00:01.230
(upbeat music)

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:02.100
- I'm gonna get that.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:03.390
This is the one I wanted.

00:00:03.390 --> 00:00:06.180
- [Narrator] Mike Shinoda
co-founded the band, Linkin Park,

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.659
one of the bestselling
acts of the 21st century.

00:00:09.659 --> 00:00:11.533
♪ One thing I don't know why ♪

00:00:11.533 --> 00:00:14.670
♪ It doesn't even matter
how hard you try ♪

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:18.000
- All my first beats were
sampled off of vinyl records.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.740
- [Narrator] Lately, he's sold a lot

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:22.770
of vinyl records and he likes to buy them.

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:23.728
- Dude.

00:00:23.728 --> 00:00:24.561
(Mike chuckling)

00:00:24.561 --> 00:00:26.190
Now that's what I call rock.

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:27.240
- [Narrator] He's not alone.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.670
Vinyl albums outsold CDs last year

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:34.203
for the first time since 1987,
to the tune of $1.2 billion.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.580
In today's digital era,
people can hear hundreds

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:42.720
of millions of songs anywhere,
and yet vinyl, expensive,

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:46.268
immobile and left for dead is booming.

00:00:46.268 --> 00:00:49.500
(record player whirring)
(upbeat music)

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:52.110
- It was kind of like, well,
yeah, that's never coming back.

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:52.943
I was wrong.

00:00:52.943 --> 00:00:53.910
- I didn't foresee it.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:58.558
- No physical formats ever
came back until this one did.

00:00:58.558 --> 00:01:00.870
(record player turning)
(record player whirring)

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:04.260
- [Narrator] Here's why this
antique format is coming back,

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:05.760
both in spite of

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.483
and because of the
digital music revolution.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:11.790
- In the beginning of the beginning,

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:14.940
around the very early
part of the 20th century,

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:18.870
there were wax cylinders
that carried sound recording.

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:20.490
- [Narrator] Thomas Edison's phonograph

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:23.670
introduced recorded music to the masses.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:26.493
Eventually, it used discs like this.

00:01:27.853 --> 00:01:31.860
(indistinct happy singing)

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:35.400
- These little black pellets
are ultimately melted down

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.280
and it becomes a biscuit that
gets pressed into a record.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.140
- [Narrator] Mark Michaels is the CEO

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.840
and owner of the largest press in America,

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.750
United Record Pressing in Nashville.

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:47.640
- Back in the '60s and '70s when vinyl

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.711
was really the only
format that was relevant

00:01:50.711 --> 00:01:53.430
and it was the core existence of United.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.440
It was a very, very busy place.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.810
- [Narrator] But the
format had its limitations.

00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:59.730
- And you couldn't really listen to it

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:02.460
on the way to work or while
you were doing anything else.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:05.460
And so CDs were far more convenient.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.730
- [Narrator] In 1987,
CDs outsold vinyl records

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:10.260
for the first time.

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.840
- We almost exclusively
listened to CDs at the time,

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.280
and vinyl, we thought of it

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.680
as like that was an old people's thing.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:18.420
- [Narrator] The format
hit its lowest point

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:23.250
in 2006 as iPods helped
digital music take over.

00:02:23.250 --> 00:02:25.770
- When I bought the company in 2007,

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:27.300
it was a much smaller business.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.520
We were 38 employees at the time.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:33.000
- [Narrator] Music lovers
had nearly unlimited options.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.560
Vinyl seemed dead.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:36.900
- Nobody thought vinyl
was gonna come back,

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.240
except for the hardest of the hardcore.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:41.970
- [Narrator] But in the
early 2010s, it did.

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:45.150
- We could ask the question,
why did that happen?

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:48.960
All of the music that has ever
been recorded is available

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:51.840
to almost all of us on our phones.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.820
- There was something
missing in that experience.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:56.010
- The album isn't just about the music.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:03:01.010
- The answer lies in the
way we express fandom.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.960
- I think a lot of fans see buying things

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:07.680
from their favorite artists as
keeping those artists going.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:11.070
- [Narrator] Meanwhile, United
watched demand skyrocket.

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:12.900
- We grew to 90 to 100 people.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:16.770
We went from running one and
a half shifts to three shifts.

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:19.410
We did everything we could
to accommodate the growth.

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:22.320
- It cost about the
same as a black t-shirt

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:24.660
at a concert and less than a hoodie.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:28.710
And it contains the actual
music and beautiful graphics

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:31.620
and photography and
lyrics and other stuff.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:34.860
And so for many fans,
especially young ones,

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:36.990
they consider that to
be a pretty good trade.

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:38.970
- [Narrator] Linkin Park's 2023 reissue

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:42.390
of their album "Meteora"
sold more than three times

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:46.860
as many vinyl copies as the
2020 reissue of "Hybrid Theory."

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:49.980
- The CD sales of "Hybrid
Theory" 20th anniversary

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:51.930
were around 13,000-ish.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:54.270
The vinyl was around 3,000ish,

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:56.220
and by the time we got to
"Meteora" two years later,

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:57.090
it flip flopped.

00:03:57.090 --> 00:04:00.510
To jump from one format so
heavily weighted to the other

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:03.390
in a couple years' time
is pretty substantial.

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:05.460
- [Narrator] And those
substantial vinyl sales

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:08.100
are helping the artists' bottom lines.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:10.890
- On-demand streaming services
like Spotify and Apple Music

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:15.060
and Amazon and and so on
are paying out something

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:17.190
well under a penny a stream.

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.680
Artists are making significantly more

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.040
on a per unit basis from record sales.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:24.600
And it stands to reason, right?

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:29.280
I mean, today a vinyl record
is gonna cost somewhere

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.490
between 25 and $30 per disk

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:35.972
- [Narrator] And many fans
prefer the sound of vinyl.

00:04:35.972 --> 00:04:39.600
(record playing static)
(lively music)

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:40.860
- There's more life to it.

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:42.030
It draws me in.

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:43.140
I don't get tired of it.

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:44.608
I wanna listen to it more.

00:04:44.608 --> 00:04:47.191
(upbeat music)

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:50.730
- If I got a record at like a garage sale

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:53.010
or something and it's an
actually, it's an old record

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:55.830
with dust and scratches
and it's got that sound,

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:58.530
then to me, it's a really
different experience.

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:00.420
- [Narrator] While vinyl is rebounding,

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:03.900
revenue from physical
formats made up just 11%

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:07.141
of recorded revenue in 2022.

00:05:07.141 --> 00:05:07.974
(upbeat music)

00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:10.950
Vinyl's continued growth isn't guaranteed

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:14.340
because records are
really hard to manufacture

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:17.700
and most machines are basically antiques.

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:19.920
- These are some of our
legacy Lenard presses.

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:23.100
Again, 50 to 60 years old
and one of the challenges

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:25.347
with these presses is,
you know, they break.

00:05:25.347 --> 00:05:27.270
- [Narrator] And that can create a backlog

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:29.220
for artists and labels.

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:32.730
- Linkin Park is not
like a fair comparison

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:35.910
because the label does
prioritize our projects.

00:05:35.910 --> 00:05:38.670
Our wait time to get the
vinyl done was six months.

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:39.780
So I have to imagine

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:42.780
for a lot of other
artists, it's a lot slower.

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:46.890
- It's very, very difficult
for an independent artist

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:51.060
or for an independent label
to schedule the manufacturing

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:54.300
and release of a relatively small run

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:56.850
of vinyl with great precision.

00:05:56.850 --> 00:05:58.680
- [Narrator] But the
industry is catching up.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:01.320
New equipment is finally being produced.

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:04.920
Mark just had 26 new presses
delivered from Sweden.

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:05.970
- Over the last two years,

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:07.980
we've been putting this together.

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:10.020
It's an $11 million project

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:13.080
- [Narrator] Now United
presses 60,000 records a day

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:16.080
and has 234 employees.

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:18.990
Despite the challenges,
some industry professionals

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:21.300
think the format is here to stay.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:26.300
- As long as there is demand
for a bespoke analog way

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:29.640
for a consumer to connect to an artist,

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:33.000
vinyl will continue to be a significant

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.187
and growing part of the business.

00:06:35.187 --> 00:06:36.020
(lively music)

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:37.350
- I don't know that it'll stick,

00:06:37.350 --> 00:06:40.080
it'll beat CDs or beat
other formats forever.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:41.550
But for now, at least people know

00:06:41.550 --> 00:06:44.160
that when they're buying an
album they love on vinyl,

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:45.480
like they're buying it for a reason.

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:48.270
It's a keepsake. It's a collectible.

00:06:48.270 --> 00:06:49.737
That's more of the mentality.

00:06:49.737 --> 00:06:52.320
(upbeat music)

